nclusion: multiples support $560\u2013675; DCF supports $620\u2013730.\n  Blended central IV = $670 (equal-weight the two methods).\n\nStep 3-5. Tornado Sensitivity (top-3 IV drivers)\n1. 2027\u201329 EPS growth (VITAS ADC + CMS rates): \u00b1$120 on IV\n2. WACC (beta/risk-free): \u00b1$80 on IV\n3. Terminal growth: \u00b1$65 on IV\n\u2192 Thesis KPIs: VITAS ADC growth; VITAS ex-cap revenue growth; WACC/rates.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3.5-1. Competitive Response\n\u2022 If VITAS sustains 8%+ growth, hospice M&A prices rise (Amedisys/\n  UnitedHealth, Compassus). Impact: \u22121 (margin pressure from labor\n  competition). Currently visible: VITAS labor costs, SG&A +5.2% in Q2.\n\u2022 Roto-Rooter brand strength triggers franchisee-led competition. Impact: 0.\n\n3.5-2. Reflexivity\n\u2022 Positive loop: rising stock \u2192 SBC cheaper \u2192 but SBC is a drag on\n  per-share metrics; activist pressure forces cost discipline \u2192 margins\n  improve \u2192 stock re-rates higher.\n\u2022 Negative loop (unlikely near-term): if Medicare cap recurred, sentiment\n  would turn; credit is ample so no funding spiral. Net: +1.\n\n3.5-3. Customer Behavior\n\u2022 Hospice: early-adopter phase is over; MAINSTREAM adoption is structural\n  (Boomers). LTV/CAC: admissions from non-hospital sources rising (+9%),\n  reducing dependence on hospital referrals \u2014 positive.\n\u2022 Roto-Rooter: consumer behavior stable; restoration/discretionary work is\n  late-cycle.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 4-1/2. Scenario Distribution (must sum to 100%)\n\u2022   Bear (25%): VITAS growth normalizes to 3\u20134% by 2027 (Medicare Cap\n    relief is non-recurring); labor costs erode margins; Roto-Rooter\n    margin falls to 19.5%; rates stay high (10Y > 5%). 2029 EPS \u2248 $27;\n    exit P/E 17x \u2192 3-yr fair value \u2248 $530.\n\u2022   Base (50%): 2026 guidance met; 2027 EPS +10% (ADC ~5%, CMS +2.3%,\n    buybacks \u22123%); fade to 5%; margins flat. DCF fair value in 3y \u2248 $740.\n\u2022   Bull (25%): VITAS ADC +6\u20137% sustains into 2028; activist (Barington)\n    drives 200bps EBITDA margin improvement; Roto-Rooter sold at 12x\n    EBITDA or unlocked; 2029 EPS \u2248 $36; P/E 20x \u2192 fair value \u2248 $875.\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian Update\n\u2022 Prior (Layer 1.5): sustained 8%+ growth base rate = 30%.\n\u2022 Evidence: Q2-2026 VITAS revenue +11.9%, ADC +6.1%, admissions +9.0%,\n  Medicare Cap liability reduced from $16.4M/Q to $0.5M/Q, CMS FY2027 +2.3%.\n  \u2192 Likelihood ratio \u2248 1.6.\n\u2022 Posterior Probability (growth > 8% in 2026): ~48% (0.30 \u00d7 1.6 / 1.0)\n  \u2192 Supports assigning a 50% probability to Base and 25% to Bull.\n\u2022 Confidence in posterior: moderate \u2014 one quarter can over-state\n  a reversal; require Q3/Q4-2026 confirmation.\n\nStep 4-4. Expected Price / MoS\n\u2022 Expected 3-yr price = 0.25($531) + 0.50($740) + 0.25($876) = $722.\n\u2022 50% CI: $600\u2013800 | 80% CI: $480\u2013900.\n\u2022 IV Base = $670 \u2192 MoS = ($670 \u2212 $540.57)/$670 = 19.3% \u21