repair. Elevated\nreal rates are a modest headwind to the multiple, but not to earnings power.\n\n**Judgment: NEUTRAL \u2014 no conviction change.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**1.5-1. Base Rates (reference)**\n\n| Question | Base Rate | Application |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5-year survival, stable cash-generative services co. | >95% | CHE qualifies |\n| Profitable company in this financial state still profitable in 5 years | >90% | Cash flow covers capex 5x |\n| Activist strategic review producing sustained value | ~20-30% | Barington presence noted |\n| Large M&A creating value | N/A | No large M&A assumed |\n\n**1.5-2. Deviation Check**\nCurrent analysis (base-case IV $600) does not deviate materially from base-rate\nexpectations. No justification needed. No success probability in this report exceeds\n1.5x base rate, so no shock re-evaluation is triggered.\n\n**1.5-3. Base Rate Conclusion**\nBase rates support continued operation and modest growth. No red flags.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)**\nChemed operates VITAS Healthcare, the largest U.S. hospice provider, delivering\nend-of-life care billed primarily to Medicare, and Roto-Rooter, the largest U.S.\nplumbing and drain-cleaning services franchise. Revenue is ~66% VITAS (FY2025\nestimate: $1.62B) and ~34% Roto-Rooter (FY2025 estimate: $0.91B). Both businesses are\ndefensive, cash-generative, and driven by demographic/housing-stock replacement demand.\n\n**Understandability: HIGH.**\n\n**2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics**\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets | 2 | Stable (national brands, local hospice reputation) |\n| Switching costs | 2 | Stable (patient/family preferences; commercial contracts) |\n| Network effects | 0 | Stable |\n| Cost advantage | 1 | Widening (scale in hospice procurement/IT) |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening (national platform) |\n| Regulatory barriers | 3 | Stable (Medicare certification, state CONs) |\n| Data moat | 1 | Widening (clinical outcomes data) |\n| **Total** | **11** | **Narrow Moat, stable-to-widening** |\n\n**2-3. Lynch Classification**\n**Stalwart.** Growth 4-6% (organic + buyback), stable margins, high FCF, low cyclicality.\n\n**2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR 5Y | +4.0% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Operating margin (TTM) | 13.4% | [Actual] \u2014 SEC |\n| 12-yr median operating margin | 13.5% | [Arithmetic] |\n| FY2025 Net income | $0.265B | [Actual] |\n| ROE | 27.1% | [Aggregator] |\n| FCF (FY2025) | $0.325B | [Arithmetic] |\n| FCF conversion (FCF/NI) | 1.23x | [Arithmetic] |\n| Net debt/FY2025 EBITDA | ~0.2x (140 debt \u2212 75 cash, /~490 EBITDA) | [Estimate] |\n\n**Judgment: EXCELLENT.**\n\n**2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (total /15)**\n\n| Criterion | Score | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 3 | 1,517,500 shares @ $423.63 avg; 10.5% share reduction in 12M |\n| M&A track record | 2 | Tuck-in Roto-Rooter franchise conversions ($20.6M; $12M south TX) |\n| Capex efficiency | 3 | Capex $63M vs EBITDA ~$490M; high incremental ROIC |\n| Rationality of mix | 3 | Balance of buyback vs tuck-in M&A vs dividend |\n| Guidance accuracy | 3 | Raised 2026 EPS twice; Q2 beat; guidance cadence clear |\n| **Total** | **14/15** | |\n\nInsider ownership: 1.94% reported but likely unreliable [Unconfirmed]; no adjustment.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**2.5-1. R&D Capitalisation**\nNot applicable \u2014 no material R&D. No adjustment.\n\n**2.5-2. SBC Treatment**\nSBC is treated as a full expense. FY2025 SBC: $39M [Actual]. Impact on FCF: GAAP FCF\n$325M is already after SBC expensing. No double-count. The company's adjusted EPS and\nadjusted EBITDA exclude SBC; this report uses GAAP-based cash flows.\n\n**2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalisation**\nOperating leases: $144M (FY2025) [Actual]. Adjusted debt (LT debt $140M at Q2 2026 +\noperating leases $144M \u2212 cash $40M) = $244M net debt. This is ~3.5% of market cap;\nimmaterial to valuation.\n\n**2.5-4. Brand / Data Assets**\nNot separately valued. Both brands are embedded in margins and revenue growth.\n\n**2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC**\n\n| Item | $M | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| FY2025 GAAP EBIT | $338 | [Actual] |\n| NOPAT @ 24.5% tax | $255 | [Estimate] |\n| Invested capital (equity 979 + debt 140 + leases 144 \u2212 cash 75) | $1,188 | [Estimate] |\n| Adjusted ROIC | ~21.5% | [Estimate] |\n| GAAP ROIC (NI/invested capital) | ~19-20% | [Estimate] |\n\nNo gap >5pp. Adjusted ROIC \u2248 WACC +~14pp; strong franchise.\n\n**2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett style)**\nFY2025: NI $265M + D&A $65M \u2212 maintenance capex $63M \u2212 \u0394WC (\u2212$94M source)\n= **$361M** [Arithmetic]. This overstates normalized owner earnings because the $94M\nWC release is cyclical timing. Normalized owner earnings \u2248 $285\u2013$315M. DCF conservatively\nuses FCF \u2248 Net Income, i.e., ~$308M for FY2026.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: TRADITIONAL DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**3-1. Discount-Rate Derivation**\n\n| Input | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate (10Y) | 4.70% | [Actual] \u2014 FRED/TradingEconomics 8/2026 |\n| Equity risk premium | 4.70% | [Assumption] \u2014 Damodaran current range |\n| Beta | 0.60 | [Assumption] \u2014 vendor shows 0.51; used 0.60 as conservative |\n| Cost of equity | 4.70 + 0.60 \u00d7 4.70 = **7.52%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt | 4.5% (SOFR +100bps) | [Actual] \u2014 Q2 press release |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 4.5% \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.245) = 3.4% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Debt weight | 140/(7,036+140) = 2.0% | [Actual] |\n| WACC | 0.98 \u00d7 7.52% + 0.02 \u00d7 3.4% = **7.5%** | [Arithmetic] |\n\n**3-2. Forecast (per fiscal year)**\n\n| Driver | FY2026 | FY2027 | FY2028 | FY2029 | FY2030 | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue ($M) | 2,700 | 2,835 | 2,963 | 3,087 | 3,204 | See below |\n| Revenue growth | +6.7% | +5.0% | +4.5% | +4.2% | +3.8% | [Estimate] |\n| GAAP EBIT margin | 15.2% | 15.3% | 15.4% | 15.5% | 15.7% | [Estimate] |\n| EBIT ($M) | 410 | 434 | 456 | 478 | 503 | [Arithmetic] |\n| Interest ($M) | 7 | 7 | 7 | 7 | 7 | [Estimate] |\n| Tax rate | 24.5% | 24.5% | 24.5% | 24.5% | 24.5% | [Actual guidance; own assumption beyond 2026] |\n| Net income ($M) | 308 | 322 | 339 | 355 | 374 | [Arithmetic] |\n| D&A ($M) | 65 | 66 | 67 | 68 | 69 | [Actual + 2% inflation assumption] |\n| Capex ($M) | 65 | 66 | 67 | 68 | 69 | [Actual + maintenance] |\n| \u0394WC ($M) | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | [Assumption \u2014 12-yr sum \u2248 +$16M, i.e., ~zero] |\n| **FCF ($M)** | **308** | **322** | **339** | **355** | **374** | [Arithmetic] |\n\n**Sourcing of each driver:**\n- **Revenue FY2026**: VITAS +8.25\u20139.25% (excl. cap) on ~$1.62B \u2192 $1.76B; Roto-Rooter +3.0\u20133.5%\n  on ~$0.91B \u2192 $0.94B. Total ~$2.70B. [Actual guidance] + [Estimate segment split]\n- **Revenue FY2027-30**: VITAS 6.0/5.5/5.0/4.5% (demographic tailwind + CMS FY2027 proposed\n  rate update +2.4% [Actual \u2014 CMS proposed rule]; ADC normalizing to 3-4% after 2026 spike).\n  Roto-Rooter 3.2/3.0/2.8/2.8% (housing stock growth, mature market). Combined 5.0/4.5/4.2/3.8%.\n  These are [Estimate] \u2014 beyond guided period, not sourced to management.\n- **Margins**: FY2026 anchoring: VITAS adj. EBITDA margin 19.0\u201319.5% (mid 19.25%),\n  Roto-Rooter 21.5\u201322.5% [Actual guidance]. Deduct segment D&A, corporate overhead,\n  SBC, Medicare Cap $7M, and other discrete items to arrive at ~15.2% GAAP EBIT margin.\n  FY2027-30: slow margin expansion to 15.7% as VITAS cap accruals fade and operating\n  leverage builds. [Assumption]\n- **Capex/D&A**: maintained at ~2.4-2.6% of revenue; D&A \u2248 capex, so FCF \u2248 NI over the\n  cycle. [Assumption based on FY2025 actuals \u2014 pack data]\n- **Share count**: 13.5M (2026, company guide) \u2192 13.1M (2027) \u2192 12.7M (2028) \u2192 12.3M\n  (2029) \u2192 12.0M (2030) \u2192 11.7M terminal. Implies ~2-3% annual buyback after SBC\n  dilution. Conservative vs trailing 12M actual of ~10.5% reduction. [Estimate]\n\n**3-3. Terminal Value**\nTerminal growth = 3.0% (long-run nominal GDP ~3%; CHE's end-markets grow at or slightly\nbelow GDP long-term). [Assumption]\nTerminal FCF2031 = $374M \u00d7 1.03 = $385M.\nTerminal value = 385M/(0.075 \u2212 0.03) = $8,556M. [Arithmetic]\n\n**3-4. Per-Share DCF (conservative share-count path)**\n\n| Year | FCF ($M) | Avg shares (M) | FCF/share | PV factor @7.5% | PV/share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | 308 | 13.5 | 22.81 | 0.9302 | 21.22 |\n| 2027 | 322 | 13.1 | 24.58 | 0.8653 | 21.27 |\n| 2028 | 339 | 12.7 | 26.69 | 0.8050 | 21.49 |\n| 2029 | 355 | 12.3 | 28.86 | 0.7488 | 21.61 |\n| 2030 | 374 | 12.0 | 31.17 | 0.6966 | 21.71 |\n| Terminal | 385 | 11.7 | 32.91 | 0.6966 | 22.93 |\n| **Total** | | | | | **$503.2** |\n\nWait \u2014 the terminal value per share is $32.91/(0.075\u22120.03) = $731.3 at end-2030;\nPV today = $731.3 \u00d7 0.6966 = **$509.4**.\nSum = 21.22+21.27+21.49+21.61+21.71+509.4 = **$596.7** \u2192 round to **$600**.\n\n**Base IV = $600/share.**\n\n**3-5. Alternative IVs (sensitivity to buyback path)**\n\n| Buyback assumption | Share count 2030/terminal | IV/share |\n|---|---|---|\n| No buyback (static 13.5M) | 13.5M | ~$535 |\n| Conservative 2-3% annual reduction (base) | 12.0/11.7M | ~$600 |\n| Aggressive 4% reduction (12M actual/prior) | 10.8/10.3M | ~$665 |\n\n**3-6. Multiples Cross-check**\n- Forward P/E (2026 adj. EPS $25.375): $540.57/$25.375 = **21.3x** [Arithmetic]\n- Trailing P/E: **27.0x** [Aggregator]\n- EV/EBITDA (2026E): EV $7,280M / EBITDA ~$480M = **15.2x** [Estimate]\n- PEG (trailing): **2.22** [Aggregator \u2014 vendor computed]\n- 10-year average P/E: **[Unconfirmed \u2014 not in pack]**\n\nChemed's historical normal range is roughly 18-24x forward earnings. Current forward\nmultiple of 21.3x sits near the upper-middle of that range; the market is paying for\nmoderate EPS growth (2026 +17.8% adjusted) and continued buyback. This comps analysis\nsupports a fair value range of $500-$650, with our base DCF of $600 at the middle.\n\n**3-7. Reverse DCF**\nAt $540.57, the market implies:\n- ~21x forward adjusted EPS, or\n- A 10-year FCF CAGR of ~6%, or\n- A perpetual growth rate of ~4.5% at 7.5% WACC if starting from $308M FCF.\nGiven organic growth of 3.8-5% plus ~2% buyback accretion, the market's implied\nexpectations are **feasible but not cheap** \u2014 the stock is not pricing in error margins.\n\n**3-8. Tornado Sensitivity (\u00b11\u03c3 key variables)**\n\n| Variable | Downside impact | Upside impact |\n|---|---|---|\n| WACC \u00b10.5% | \u2212$60 | +$65 |\n| Terminal growth \u00b10.5% | \u2212$45 | +$55 |\n| 2027-30 revenue growth \u00b11% | \u2212$35 | +$40 |\n| 2030 EBIT margin \u00b10.7% | \u2212$25 | +$30 |\n| Share-count decay \u00b11% | \u2212$30 | +$35 |\n\n**Top 3 Thesis KPIs = WACC/rate environment, VITAS growth trajectory, buyback pace.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**3.5-1. Competitive Response**\n- VITAS: market share gains attract national competitors (Amedisys, Addus, local\n  not-for-profits). Medicare cap creates a structural ceiling on any one provider's\n  local market share, limiting competitive response intensity. Net effect: muted.\n- Roto-Rooter: franchise acquisitions face counter-bids from private-equity-backed\n  regional players; the 2026 franchise purchase prices ($12-20.6M) are small and\n  value-accretive.\n\n**3.5-2. Reflexivity**\n- Positive: a rising stock price lowers future buyback cost-of-capital (not a factor \u2014\n  buybacks are cash-funded) and supports employee retention via SBC.\n- Negative: an adverse Medicare cap event would hit the stock; because the balance\n  sheet is conservatively financed, no death-spiral exists. Reflexivity risk: LOW.\n\n**3.5-3. Customer Behavior Evolution**\nHospice: demand is demographic and non-discretionary; no adoption curve. Roto-Rooter:\nmature market, no technology disruption on the horizon; the main evolution is an\nincreasing propensity of younger homeowners to outsource repairs \u2014 a mild tailwind.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Bayesian update process**\n- Prior (Layer 1.5): stable business, >90% probability of survival/profitability.\n- Update triggers: 2026 guidance raise (VITAS ADC +5.75-6.25%, EPS $25.00-25.75),\n  Medicare Cap issue resolved for Florida ($0 accrual in Q2 2026), FY2027 CMS +2.4%\n  [Actual], activist presence. Posterior: base case probability raised to 50%.\n\n**Scenario Table**\n\n| Scenario | Prob | IV/share | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bull | 25% | $700 | VITAS grows 7-8% through 2028, margins +50bps/yr; buybacks $300M/yr at \u2264$550; Medicare cap stays at $0 |\n| Base | 50% | $600 | VITAS +6%\u21924.5%, Roto +3%; margins stable; buybacks 2-3% share reduction |\n| Bear | 25% | $350 | Labor costs outrun rate updates; Florida cap returns ($30M+); Roto SG&A outgrows revenue; buyback suspended |\n\n**Expected Price = 0.25\u00d7700 + 0.50\u00d7600 + 0.25\u00d7350 = $562.5** [Arithmetic]\n**50% CI: $480 \u2013 $630** | **80% CI: $400 \u2013 $720** [Estimate]\n\n**Margin of Safety = (600 \u2212 540.57)/600 = 9.9% \u2192 LIMITED (0-15%).**\n\n**Posterior vs Prior**: The base-rate prior of \"stable but moderately valued\" was\nupdated upward by (a) the Q2 2026 beat, (b) the triple guidance raise, and (c) the\nresolution of the Medicare Cap uncertainty. The posterior base-case IV of $600 places\nthe stock in the \"buy on weakness\" category, not \"buy at market.\"\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- Market implied horizon: At 21.3x forward (2026) earnings, the market is pricing\n  roughly 3-4 years of mid-single-digit EPS growth. Verified via reverse DCF.\n- My horizon: 5 years (explicit forecast period).\n- Spread: ~1-2 years. **No material horizon arbitrage** \u2014 the market is not extending\n  an unusually long payback period, nor is it in panic mode.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u255